  "reply": "The latest stable release is Python 3.15.0, released October 9, 2026 ([python.org](https://blog.python.org/2026/10/python-3150-final-is-here/)).\n\nIts headline changes:\n\n- **Lazy Imports:** Modules load only when first used, which speeds up startup by an estimated 15 to 20% ([dev.to](https://dev.to/romdevin/python-315-released-new-features-improvements-and-resources-for-developers-2dgf)).\n- **UTF-8 as default encoding:** Standard text I/O now defaults to UTF-8, which makes behavior more consistent across platforms ([plainenglish.io](https://python.plainenglish.io/python-3-15-is-officially-launched-7-changes-that-actually-matter-for-developers-0394bb2d4987)).\n- **New built-ins:** `frozendict` (an immutable dictionary) and `sentinel` (for distinct special values) ([bacancytechnology.com](https://www.bacancytechnology.com/blog/python-3-15)).\n- **Unpacking in comprehensions:** This simplifies flattening nested data ([bacancytechnology.com](https://www.bacancytechnology.com/blog/python-3-15)).\n- **Performance work:** The release includes a JIT compiler upgrade and the Tachyon sampling profiler for lower-overhead profiling ([dev.to](https://dev.to/romdevin/python-315-released-new-features-improvements-and-resources-for-developers-2dgf)).\n\nFor the full list, see the [official 3.15 what's new page](https://docs.python.org/3/whatsnew/3.15.html).",
