  "message": "What does published research say about whether running volume or hill training raises knee injury risk in recreational marathon trainees? Please cite a few papers.",
  "reply": "I found no published study that directly tests whether running volume or hill training raises knee injury risk in recreational marathon trainees. I read only titles and abstracts, not full papers, so these are the closest matches:\n\n- **Dos Santos et al. (2026)**, a narrative review of [running volume and knee osteoarthritis in adult runners](https://doi.org/10.56238/sevened2026.009-061). The abstract cuts off before its conclusions, so I can't say what it finds.\n- **Leaver (1999)**, a [case-control study of total running volume and osteoarthritis](http://hdl.handle.net/11427/26670) in weight-bearing joints. It studied ultra-distance runners, so it applies only loosely.\n- **Perugini et al. (2025)**, a [review of lower-limb injuries in marathon running](https://doi.org/10.1007/s12178-025-10000-z). I saw only the title, not an abstract.\n- **Zhao et al. (2025)**, an [MRI study of 14 amateur marathon runners](https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.19123). It measured knee fat-pad changes after a marathon but did not measure injury risk.\n\nYour sore left knee after hills is worth raising at your physio visit, since these papers can't assess your knee.",
